{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791211932,"nonce":"XL226EedwIV4ZybtGWTHszj9/mw=","title":"Array\u003cNull\u003cInt\u003e\u003e of charCodeAt values stores raw ints, typed read unboxes them","message":"var s = \"GGCC\";\n    var a = [s.charCodeAt(0)];      // also [c], [for (i in 0...2) s.charCodeAt(i)]\n    var x:Int = a[0];               // SIGSEGV fault=0x47 ('G') outside JIT code\n\ncharCodeAt yields a raw Int register typed Null\u003cInt\u003e, so the array is Array\u003cNull\u003cInt\u003e\u003e (correct Haxe typing) but its elements are stored unboxed. Reading an element into Int opens it as a Null\u003cInt\u003e box and dereferences the char code. Printing the element (`\"\" + a[0]`) happens to work, which hides it.\n\nThe store side should box a Null\u003cscalar\u003e value that is not already a box (boxed_value_regs), or the element type should follow the value's representation; keying on the declared type alone has cost rows before (memory: decayed Null/Dynamic rules).\n\nFound while adding the fasta benchmark kernel (an unannotated `[for (i in 0...src.length) src.charCodeAt(i)]` in an instance method). The kernel now uses an Array\u003cInt\u003e-returning helper like the other FASTA kernels. Probes F2/F3 in the session notes.","files":null}]}